1
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:38,380
CONVERSATION 1
Welcome to the Book Club.

2
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:47,620
Today's guest is Susan Lane, the author of a new book on personal finances that has already sold half a million copies.

3
00:00:47,620 --> 00:00:51,540
Hi, Susan, your book is extremely successful.

4
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:52,660
Why do you think that is?

5
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:59,780
I think that's because of my message, which is making happiness a priority over money.

6
00:00:59,780 --> 00:01:09,880
So many of us in my generation have spent decades trying to earn more money, just to consume more, but it made us more miserable.

7
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:19,200
You yourself were once caught in that cycle, working for two decades as an executive and earning a high salary, but still accumulating debt.

8
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:21,360
I most certainly was.

9
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:30,020
I earned millions, but by the time I quit my job four years ago to become a writer, I owed over $ 30,000.

10
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:32,560
So how did you escape that pattern?

11
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:35,280
And what would you advise other people to do?

12
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:38,120
The first change is in what we value.

13
00:01:38,120 --> 00:01:46,340
We need to emphasize things that actually make us happy, like relationships, the environment or even our hobbies.

14
00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:53,440
Once we make the right things our priority, our goals will change and so will our financial behavior.

15
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:56,880
How does that translate into practical action?

16
00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:01,780
Can you give our audience examples of what you are talking about on an everyday basis?

17
00:02:01,780 --> 00:02:07,660
The major areas for action are usually housing, food and transportation.

18
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:19,500
So people might share a home with friends instead of living on their own, bring lunch from home instead of going to restaurants, and use public transport instead of owning a car.

19
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:22,200
Those sound like major sacrifices.

20
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:24,340
I could never share my home.

21
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:26,280
I need my own space.

22
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:28,320
But they aren't sacrifices.

23
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:31,840
When people change their values, their desires change.

24
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:42,840
So in the example of housing, if we value relationships, sharing a home isn't depriving ourselves of space, but giving us an opportunity to spend more time with our loved ones.

25
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:52,300
Indeed it is.

26
00:02:52,300 --> 00:03:09,980
Q1. What message does the woman convey in her book?

27
00:03:09,980 --> 00:03:28,040
Q2. What do we learn about the woman before she became a writer?

28
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:46,060
Q3. What does the woman say about one's financial behavior?

29
00:03:46,060 --> 00:04:03,180
Q4. What does the man say about sharing a home?

30
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:07,060
CONVERSATION 2
Thank you for inviting me to the gallery, Christopher.

31
00:04:07,060 --> 00:04:11,120
I haven't visited here since your predecessor's retirement functioned.

32
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:15,840
Would you like to see the newest additions to our collection first, Catherine?

33
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:18,860
Are those the landscapes by Daniel Gregory?

34
00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:21,760
I absolutely adore her work.

35
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:27,920
This first piece was a gift to the gallery from the artist herself, and it's quite exquisite.

36
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:31,040
I love how she depicts the barren landscape.

37
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:34,020
The colors complement each other perfectly.

38
00:04:34,020 --> 00:04:37,200
You can sense the desolation in the picture.

39
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:41,760
This piece was inspired by Gregory's recent trek in the Gobi Desert.

40
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:45,220
And how did you obtain her other piece, over here?

41
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:51,780
It was purchased at auction by an anonymous collector who lent it to the gallery for display.

42
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:55,620
This composition is one of her most acclaimed paintings.

43
00:04:55,620 --> 00:04:59,780
It must have cost that collector a small fortune to purchase this.

44
00:04:59,780 --> 00:05:00,640
Obviously.

45
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:05,920
I can't disclose the exact amount he paid, but it was substantial.

46
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:08,220
There' s so much detail in this painting.

47
00:05:08,220 --> 00:05:11,440
I feel like I can really immerse myself in the scene.

48
00:05:11,440 --> 00:05:16,880
I particularly like the symmetry created by the reflection of the mountain in the lake.

49
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:21,900
This particular piece was the one that was nominated for a Gateway Award.

50
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:26,520
I was lucky enough to attend he awards ceremony as Gregory's guest.

51
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:28,700
So you know her personally.

52
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:31,420
I assume she is an eccentric artist.

53
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:32,940
Quite the opposite.

54
00:05:32,940 --> 00:05:35,740
In fact, she's not at all eccentric.

55
00:05:35,740 --> 00:05:40,260
I would say she's one of the most easygoing and intelligent people I know.

56
00:05:40,260 --> 00:05:42,180
I'd love to be able to meet her.

57
00:05:42,180 --> 00:05:44,800
There are so many questions I'd like to ask.

58
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:46,240
What a coincidence!

59
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:48,600
I'm meeting her for dinner tonight.

60
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:49,800
Would you like to come along?

61
00:05:49,800 --> 00:05:50,900
I'd love to.

62
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:58,940
Thank you.

63
00:05:58,940 --> 00:06:18,680
Q5. What do we learn about one of the newest additions to the gallery's collection?

64
00:06:18,680 --> 00:06:38,780
Q6. What does the man say about one of the most acclaimed paintings by Daniel Gregory?

65
00:06:38,780 --> 00:06:57,580
Q7. Why does the woman say she can feel immersed in the scene in the painting?

66
00:06:57,580 --> 00:07:42,640
Q8. How does the man describe Daniel Gregory?

67
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:46,480
PASSAGE 1
Forgiveness is the release of resentment or anger.

68
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:49,420
Forgiveness doesn't mean reconciliation.

69
00:07:49,420 --> 00:07:57,320
We don't have to return to the same relationship, nor do we have to accept the same harmful behaviors from an offender.

70
00:07:57,320 --> 00:08:02,400
Forgiveness is vitally important for the mental health of certain victims.

71
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:10,340
It propels people forward rather than keeping them emotionally engaged in an injustice or trauma.

72
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:16,100
Carrying the hurt or anger of an offense leads the body to release stress chemicals.

73
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:24,680
Eliminating the perpetual flow of those chemicals may also explain why forgiveness provides physical health benefits.

74
00:08:24,680 --> 00:08:28,840
There are scenarios in which forgiveness is not the best course.

75
00:08:28,840 --> 00:08:34,780
Sometimes the victim becomes more empowered when they give themselves permission not to forgive.

76
00:08:34,780 --> 00:08:36,900
Forgiveness can be challenging.

77
00:08:36,900 --> 00:08:46,060
This is especially true when the offending party offers an insincere apology, or maybe they haven't offered anything at all.

78
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:50,320
However, it's often the healthiest path forward.

79
00:08:50,320 --> 00:09:05,140
It's important to cultivate forgiveness by developing compassion for the offender, reflect on whether the act was due to malicious intent, or whether it was caused by challenging circumstances in the offender's life.

80
00:09:05,140 --> 00:09:07,800
What about forgiving ourselves?

81
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:16,660
We sometimes need to take responsibility for mistakes, but intense guilt and shame aren't a desirable outcome in the long run.

82
00:09:16,660 --> 00:09:20,560
Forgiving yourself may seem like an ambiguous process.

83
00:09:20,560 --> 00:09:31,270
You can begin by acknowledging that you are at fault, take responsibility for the hurt you caused, then reflect on why the event occurred.

84
00:09:31,270 --> 00:09:46,330
Draw the lessons you learned and try to avoid committing a similar offense in the future.

85
00:09:46,330 --> 00:10:01,390
Q9. What does the passage say about forgiveness?

86
00:10:01,390 --> 00:10:18,750
Q10. When is forgiveness especially challenging?

87
00:10:18,750 --> 00:10:36,090
Q11. What should one do in order to forgive the offender?

88
00:10:36,090 --> 00:10:43,110
PASSAGE 2
The Glasgow subway first opened in 1896 as a cable-hauled system.

89
00:10:43,110 --> 00:10:49,990
It is generally recognized as the world's third underground railway, after London and Budapest.

90
00:10:49,990 --> 00:10:57,850
In its long history, it has never been expanded remaining as a single loop line with a mere 15 stations.

91
00:10:57,850 --> 00:11:03,970
At its peak, it served the shipyard workers on the south side of the city.

92
00:11:03,970 --> 00:11:11,930
In the 1960s, there was a decline in the shipbuilding industry, and the popularity of private transport grew.

93
00:11:11,930 --> 00:11:17,070
As a result, the subway saw a rapid decline in ridership.

94
00:11:17,070 --> 00:11:27,450
It ran with little further change until 1977 when its new operators closed it for major modernization investment.

95
00:11:27,450 --> 00:11:40,030
Carriages were replaced, ventilation was improved, and the main depot was also renovated and fitted with connecting tracks to replace the outdated crane transfer mechanism.

96
00:11:40,030 --> 00:11:46,770
The subway in its present form reopened for operation in April 1980.

97
00:11:46,770 --> 00:11:51,790
Since its relaunch, the subway has seen a revival in its fortunes.

98
00:11:51,790 --> 00:12:01,210
It serves as a viable alternative to other forms of transport and has gone a long way to alleviate traffic jams in the city center.

99
00:12:01,210 --> 00:12:07,450
In 1996, the system reached an important milestone-100 years.

100
00:12:07,450 --> 00:12:13,530
To commemorate this special event, the color scheme of the train carriages was updated.

101
00:12:13,530 --> 00:12:22,950
Recently, high-tech systems such as smart card ticketing machines and smart gates are used across all subway stations.

102
00:12:22,950 --> 00:12:33,070
The smart card provides more convenient travel and passengers simply top up their cards and tap them to get in and out the subway.

103
00:12:33,070 --> 00:12:47,790
Since its launch, the technology has been adopted by more than 100,000 subway customers.

104
00:12:47,790 --> 00:13:06,970
Q12. What does the passage say about the Glasgow subway in the 1960s?

105
00:13:06,970 --> 00:13:25,610
Q13. Why was the Glasgow subway closed in 1977?

106
00:13:25,610 --> 00:13:42,790
Q14. Why does the Glasgow subway remained important today?

107
00:13:42,790 --> 00:14:28,550
Q15. What does the passage say about all subway stations in Glasgow nowadays?

108
00:14:28,550 --> 00:14:37,790
RECORDING 1
If you visit the Great Smoky Mountains National Park, chances are you will remember the roadside or campground bears above all else.

109
00:14:37,790 --> 00:14:43,050
Bears are the most popular animals in a number of our national parks.

110
00:14:43,050 --> 00:14:54,090
In these mountains, where the population of bears runs into the hundreds, opportunities to observe these large wild animals are plentiful during the summer.

111
00:14:54,090 --> 00:15:06,310
Since national parks are wildlife sanctuaries where no disturbance of the native animals is allowed, years of protection have served to break down the wild bears' fear of humans.

112
00:15:06,310 --> 00:15:15,230
Now, instead of depending on their own resources for a living, many bears patrol park roads and campgrounds.

113
00:15:15,230 --> 00:15:18,950
They give the garbage cans a frequent going over.

114
00:15:18,950 --> 00:15:27,550
An occasional offer of food from a park visitor, an illegal and dangerous practice, makes beggars of them.

115
00:15:27,550 --> 00:15:37,190
Bears are very often hungry and since they will feed on almost any kind of plant or animal, garbage is quite acceptable.

116
00:15:37,190 --> 00:15:49,490
Feeding them, however, represents misguided kindness because the bears come to expect such generosity from everyone and consequently, trouble could lie ahead.

117
00:15:49,490 --> 00:15:52,430
Park rules prohibit the feeding of bears.

118
00:15:52,430 --> 00:15:54,650
Violators are arrested.

119
00:15:54,650 --> 00:16:02,630
Every year, doctors who have offices near the park treat a number of cases of bear bites and bear scratches.

120
00:16:02,630 --> 00:16:06,730
Some of the accidents have come about in strange ways.

121
00:16:06,730 --> 00:16:15,390
One man was in the process of feeding two small cubs, when the mother bear appeared and insisted upon having some of the food.

122
00:16:15,390 --> 00:16:24,910
Shoving the big bear aside with one hand, the man continued feeding the cubs when suddenly he was struck a fierce blow in the face.

123
00:16:24,910 --> 00:16:32,770
A bear, prompted by the food that a lady kept offering to him, entered the car where the generous person was sitting.

124
00:16:32,770 --> 00:16:37,850
Her efforts to push the bear out of the car resulted in injuries.

125
00:16:37,850 --> 00:16:44,430
A man required medical attention after he applied a lighted cigarette to a bear's nose.

126
00:16:44,430 --> 00:16:54,810
Another man tried to boost a bear into the front seat of his car so that he might take a picture of a bear sitting beside his wife who was behind the wheel.

127
00:16:54,810 --> 00:17:11,150
Because bears prefer roads and campgrounds, the possibility of a hiker meeting up with a bear' along park trails is small, but there's always that chance, for a bear seems to know if you are carrying a lunch·or a candy bar.

128
00:17:11,150 --> 00:17:22,250
He may even insist on taking it.

129
00:17:22,250 --> 00:17:41,670
Q16. What does the speaker say about the bears in national parks now?

130
00:17:41,670 --> 00:18:01,570
Q17. What does the speaker say about visitors feeding bears in national parks?

131
00:18:01,570 --> 00:18:22,850
Q18. What is the speaker's advice to people who carry some food while hiking on a park trail?

132
00:18:22,850 --> 00:18:29,930
RECORDING 2
Why do we form opinions or attitudes about someone or something without really knowing much about them?

133
00:18:29,930 --> 00:18:38,810
Just hearing something good or bad about a person, a place or thing can influence our opinions positively or negatively.

134
00:18:38,810 --> 00:18:45,630
But letting the opinions of another person determine what our opinions will be is dangerous.

135
00:18:45,630 --> 00:18:52,250
Forming opinions about someone or something before really knowing them well is called prejudice.

136
00:18:52,250 --> 00:18:56,810
"Pre-" means before and "-judice" refers to judgment.

137
00:18:56,810 --> 00:19:02,430
Hence, prejudice means to judge before having adequate knowledge.

138
00:19:02,430 --> 00:19:06,530
We can be prejudiced toward or against someone or something.

139
00:19:06,530 --> 00:19:12,230
In either case, we are only allowing ourselves to see half of the picture.

140
00:19:12,230 --> 00:19:18,290
Very few people or things in this world are all good or all bad.

141
00:19:18,290 --> 00:19:31,430
Prejudiced attitudes are usually based on myths, half truths or incorrect information, and they are dangerous because they can keep us from learning the truth about someone or something.

142
00:19:31,430 --> 00:19:41,750
People form prejudices against others for many reasons-differences in their race, religion, gender or occupation.

143
00:19:41,750 --> 00:19:44,350
Prejudices keep people apart.

144
00:19:44,350 --> 00:19:48,670
They keep us from really knowing and understanding each other.

145
00:19:48,670 --> 00:19:53,590
We should feel proud of who we are and the group of people we represent.

146
00:19:53,590 --> 00:20:07,410
If feelings of pride begin to turn to feelings of superiority when we think that our group or our beliefs are better than those around us, however, then we begin to develop prejudiced attitudes that can be harmful.

147
00:20:07,410 --> 00:20:20,490
For example, the prejudiced attitudes of one group may keep another group from attending certain schools, from living in any neighborhood they want, or from getting a job or a promotion.

148
00:20:20,490 --> 00:20:25,830
Extreme feelings of prejudice have caused the deaths of innocent people.

149
00:20:25,830 --> 00:20:29,350
We are responsible for our own thoughts and opinions.

150
00:20:29,350 --> 00:20:37,390
When we let someone else tell us what to think about someone or something, we are giving up some control of our own lives.

151
00:20:37,390 --> 00:20:44,910
Before you form an attitude or opinion, find out for yourself about the person or the thing in question.

152
00:20:44,910 --> 00:20:51,590
Sometimes we don't realize that we hold prejudiced attitudes toward or against someone or something.

153
00:20:51,590 --> 00:21:05,330
We need to carefully examine our lives and our fears, and to ask ourselves whether our attitudes come from our personal knowledge and experience or from rumors and fear of the unknown.

154
00:21:05,330 --> 00:21:09,430
The good news about prejudice is that we are not born with it.

155
00:21:09,430 --> 00:21:13,330
Prejudiced attitudes and opinions develop over time.

156
00:21:13,330 --> 00:21:27,190
But with education and knowledge, we can replace our prejudices with cooperation and understanding.

157
00:21:27,190 --> 00:21:44,450
Q19. What does the speaker say about prejudice?

158
00:21:44,450 --> 00:22:03,170
Q20. Why does the speaker say prejudiced attitudes are dangerous?

159
00:22:03,170 --> 00:22:22,690
Q21. When does the speaker say we begin to develop prejudiced attitudes?

160
00:22:22,690 --> 00:22:26,250
RECORDING 3
When I started high school, it was a shock.

161
00:22:26,250 --> 00:22:32,490
I had spent eight years fighting my way to be the most popular kid in the Catholic school student body.

162
00:22:32,490 --> 00:22:44,190
I had been a big, tough 8th grader and suddenly I was a lonely 9th grader bullied by the big, tough 12th grade seniors who ran the high school.

163
00:22:44,190 --> 00:22:54,350
I realized then that it's nice to strive for something, but that you also have to enjoy the moment you're in and be happy where you are.

164
00:22:54,350 --> 00:22:57,550
Rock and roll had always been an important part of my life.

165
00:22:57,550 --> 00:23:06,790
I remember my friends and I used to drive around until the late hours of the nights listening to the music of Rock and Roll Legends.

166
00:23:06,790 --> 00:23:12,930
During those teenage years, I built friendships that I thought would last a lifetime.

167
00:23:12,930 --> 00:23:18,890
Most people that age think the same thing, but people drift apart.

168
00:23:18,890 --> 00:23:24,750
Jobs, families and tragedies separate people from those lasting friendships.

169
00:23:24,750 --> 00:23:31,090
The tragedy that separated me from my friends forever was the Vietnam war.

170
00:23:31,090 --> 00:23:36,070
A year after I graduated from high school, I left for Vietnam.

171
00:23:36,070 --> 00:23:47,750
I came back, burned out, and tired, as though I had lived 10 lifetimes in the short span of 14 months, the 14 months I was in the war.

172
00:23:47,750 --> 00:23:50,550
I couldn't relate to the friends I had had in high school.

173
00:23:50,550 --> 00:23:56,530
They still seem childish, concerned with childish things that weren't important to me.

174
00:23:56,530 --> 00:24:03,350
I was still trying to cope with the death, destruction and evil I had seen in Vietnam.

175
00:24:03,350 --> 00:24:11,570
I felt like we had done terrible things to innocent people there and in turn, I had seen terrible things done to my friends.

176
00:24:11,570 --> 00:24:15,030
I withdrew for my friends and started college.

177
00:24:15,030 --> 00:24:18,730
Then I quit college and took many different jobs.

178
00:24:18,730 --> 00:24:23,950
I spent a lot of my time and money on alcohol and other drugs.

179
00:24:23,950 --> 00:24:35,870
Finally, in an effort to get my life going in the right direction again, I sold everything and took what little money I had and bought myself an airplane ticket to Israel.

180
00:24:35,870 --> 00:24:38,550
I went there to study history.

181
00:24:38,550 --> 00:24:45,790
While studying at Haifa University, I met my wife, who was also an American student.

182
00:24:45,790 --> 00:24:49,390
I now teach in a high school back in America.

183
00:24:49,390 --> 00:24:56,070
I look at my students and see them struggling with many of the very things I struggled with many years ago.

184
00:24:56,070 --> 00:25:10,030
As a teacher, I try to help them over the rough spots as best I can.

185
00:25:10,030 --> 00:25:28,430
Q22. How did the speaker feel when he started high school?

186
00:25:28,430 --> 00:25:47,770
Q23. What did the speaker once think of teenage friendships?

187
00:25:47,770 --> 00:26:05,950
Q24. What do we learn about the speaker when he returned from the Vietnam war?

188
00:26:05,950 --> 00:26:26,024
Q25. What does the speaker try to do as a teacher?
